  "final_answer_preview": "Murder, as defined in Section 101 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, occurs when a person causes death with the intention of causing death, with the intention of causing bodily injury likely to cause death, or with knowledge that their act is likely to cause death. Culpable homicide, defined in Section 100, encompasses a broader range of actions that result in death but may not involve the same level of intent or knowledge. For instance, culpable homicide can occur if a person causes death without the intention to kill or without knowing that their actions are likely to cause death, especially in cases of provocation or accidents. Thus, while all murders are culpable homicides, not all culpable homicides qualify as murder.",
